  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "A sun-synchronous orbit maintains approximately constant local solar time, not continuous sunlight. The claim that the satellite has a constant supply of sunlight and that the descending path is constantly sunlit is not established; eclipse periods depend on orbital geometry and local time of the node."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The 600 km altitude and 97.79° inclination are plausible for a sun-synchronous orbit, but no sensor, spectral bands, ground sample distance, signal-to-noise analysis, or water-depth capability is given to establish that fluid-lensing reef imagery is feasible."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The report says a 250 km reef width motivates the altitude choice, but then proposes imaging sections without calculating swath, revisit, or time to cover the reef. The statement that low image resolution is desirable conflicts with the stated need for useful analysis."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "RAAN specifies the orientation of the orbital plane, not the launch facility's location. The initial RAAN and argument of latitude lack an epoch or specified local time of the ascending node, so the initial orbit geometry is not fully defined."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish that it has spacecraft command and telemetry equipment, suitable frequencies, or a data link budget. No data volume or transfer-rate calculation supports the claim that contacts are sufficient."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The access table duplicates the same access as entries 14 and 15, so the reported access statistics may double-count a pass. Its multi-day output also does not establish a clear operational communication cadence."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "A drag sail is proposed without sizing, deployment reliability, or an estimated decay time from 600 km. The report therefore does not demonstrate that passive disposal meets an acceptable post-mission lifetime."
    },
    {
      "where": "Launch",
      "issue": "The report selects a future Vega E rideshare concept rather than identifying a confirmed launch opportunity or showing that its deployment service can deliver this spacecraft into the specified orbit."
    },
    {
      "where": "Bibliography",
      "issue": "Wikipedia is cited as a source despite the task explicitly rejecting it as a proper source. Several table citations numbered [50] and above do not correspond to entries in the bibliography, which contains only references [1]–[10]."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "The report presents a coherent mission concept and covers most required design topics, with useful STK screenshots and access outputs. Its principal weaknesses are unverified reef-imaging feasibility, incomplete communications and disposal analyses, and presentation and referencing defects. The selected orbit is plausible, but several operational claims are unsupported or inaccurate."
}
